Step 5 – Memory Dump Analysis
BSODs generate a .dmp file in C:\Windows\Minidump
> Locate the dump file - C:\Windows\Minidump

 When a BSOD occurs, Windows saves a snapshot of the system memory (a “minidump”) at the moment of the crash. This file contains clues about what caused the crash like the driver, hardware, or system module that failed.

[screenshot6.png] 
Note: The Minidump folder is empty because the system has not experienced a BSOD, so Windows has not generated any crash dump files.

The goal of this step is to analyze a Windows crash dump file to determine what component caused a hypothetical Blue Screen of Death (BSOD). Windows typically saves these crash reports as .dmp files in:
C:\Windows\Minidump
These files contain a snapshot of system memory at the moment the crash occurred. They act almost like an autopsy report for its system failure, allowing us to trace back which driver, module, or kernel component triggered the crash.

Step 5 – Open the dump with a tool
Analyze the dump using WinDbg or BlueScreenView to identify the driver or module causing the crash.
Two common tools can analyze these files: WinDbg and BlueScreenView. I chose to use WinDbg, which is Microsoft’s official debugging tool and provides more detailed analysis. 
Since WinDbg already offers the necessary functionality and deeper debugging capabilities, installing an additional tool would have been redundant for this exercise.

